what do a goldfish driving a robot on  land a rat cruising around in a lab and  a california girl trekking around in the  snow have in common we're all doing  things that don't come naturally to us  and still we figured out how to succeed  in unfamiliar situations and  environments how  it has to do with what's up here and the  fact that we're not hard-headed quite  the opposite actually  mental flexibility is critical to all  brains and really to our survival but  before we get into the wonderful world  of brains neuroplasticity and learning  let's set indoors because it's freezing  out here  so now that we're settled in let's start  with the goldfish  what you're seeing here is it's robo  ride down the side of a street but  before the fish was let loose it had to  learn to drive indoors as part of a  recent experiment to test whether fish  actually had the brain power to learn to  drive a vehicle and that answer was a  resounding yes so a goldfish can drive  why does that even matter adaptation to  new environment and  new ways of advancing in space this is  very important for all animals not just  for fish but also for us that's roman  seguin the neuroscientists who train the  fish to drive the brain's plasticity or  flexibility to adapt is critical for our  survival it's what allows us to come up  with creative solutions to hard problems  like finding friends or food or avoiding  threats like predators and the extreme  cold by studying animals scientists can  get insights into how different brains  react to new challenges and what their  limits might be that's important in two  ways for understanding the evolution of  brains generally and for understanding  what goes wrong when brains malfunction  the goldfish and rodents experiment had  to learn to steer a robotic vehicle  towards a pink target in a terrestrial  environment ronan compared the  experience of his fish to humans in  outer space where humans have to learn  to move in zero gravity sometimes inside  cumbersome spacesuits  his goldfish had to figure out their  position in the tank controlled the way  the vehicle moved it's not unlike when  we learn to ride a bike or drive a car  we know that this is actually a very  complex motor task and the fish didn't  just manage they adapted and thrived  they got so good that about a dozen  driving sessions in they were getting  full lunch from this experiment  each time the fish hit their pink target  they got a bit of fish food remember  that word because we're going to come  back to it later even when the  researchers moved the target or added  decoys the fish still made their way to  the food it's not an accidental just  bumping into the target due to some  random motion over time their path to  lunch became more direct they really  knew where they were going this fish  driving experiment and another test i'm  about to tell you in just a sec are  important for two reasons first animals  are smart maybe smarter than we give  them credit for in some ways they're  kind of super achievers that's kelly  lambert a neuroscientist who studies  stress anxiety and resilience but in her  experiments rats rather than fish are  the stars the fact that animal brains  are capable of some pretty complex  behaviors gives researchers like kelly  more confidence that we can learn  something about ourselves by studying  other animals that's important because  researchers often use animal brains as  proxies for ours so knowing how far they  can be challenged is important for  improving the basic research that guides  clinical development it's still not  perfect because rats are not little  humans and we're not big brats  but it's a starting point i promised you  a second insight so here it is  motivation and reward is important for  learning and adapting for ronan's  goldfish it was fish food and in kelly's  lab our rats love fruit i don't know if  that's a good endorsement for fruit loop  cereal but  they'll work for that incidentally fruit  loops maker kellogg's did not respond to  a request for comment over the course of  months her team coaxed the rats to take  up driving by rewarding them with sugary  cereal in the process her team noticed  an interesting difference between the  two sets of rats they were working with  one group lived in standard cages the  other rats had bigger cages with toys  and other rats to play with pura vida  edition the animals in the enriched  environment learned faster meaning it  took them fewer sessions to go from the  start line to the finish line where they  were rewarded with more food loops we  see something similar in humans we know  that having access to education  resources and nice living environment  when we're kids that all sets us up for  success later in life as for the rats  the researchers also noticed another  interesting difference once the rats  were familiarized with their ride kelly  noticed that the privileged rats grow  further even without the fruit loop  stimulus  something about their brains that were  different that allowed kelly doesn't  know what the difference was  anatomically but she wants to take a  look at how the connections between  brain cells change as rats learn to  drive neurons are so wonderful like  they're trees with roots and connections  so  those existing neurons can  be plugged into more circuits the  strengthening of neural connections and  the sprouting of new ones is fundamental  for brain plasticity even without  knowing these details it's clear that  something about the rats prior  experience shaped their perception of  reward and made them either less or more  prone to explore  neuroscientists know that novelty can be  rewarding too so maybe the rats in the  fancy cages were more attuned to that  and their brains were already primed to  experiment and adapt so what does this  mean for us when we look closer at the  fish and the rats driving they're really  showing us neuroscience in action  they're evidence that our 